  <div class="modal-dialog modal-dialog-centered modal-dialog-scrollable" style="max-width:800px;">
    <div class="modal-content">
      <div class="modal-header">
        <div class="d-flex align-items-center">
            <img src="https://pg.concordreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/hospital-pin-red.png" style="width:30px; height:30px;">
            <h5 class="modal-title" id="disclaimerLabel">Territorial Hospital</h5>
        </div>
         <button type="button" class="btn-close" data-bs-dismiss="modal" aria-label="Close"></button>
      </div>
      <div class="modal-body">
        <p class="p-modal text-justify">
            The territorial level is the top of the New Caledonian system and serves the whole territory. It consists of two public hospital establishments in Greater Nouméa. The first is the Centre hospitalier territorial Gaston-Bourret (CHT, Territorial Hospital Center), the territory’s general and reference hospital, which has been regrouped on the Médipôle de Koutio site in Dumbéa. The second is the Centre hospitalier spécialisé Albert-Bousquet (CHS, Specialized Hospital Center) in Nouville, Nouméa, which provides psychiatric and geriatric care. Under the emergency-care scheme annexed to Deliberation 171, the CHT’s emergency department is the only Niveau 4 – niveau de référence (reference level) service. All specialized medical, surgical, obstetric and psychiatric emergency care is routed to it, and evacuations outside the territory are organized from it.
        </p>
        <p class="p-modal text-justify">
            <strong>Note:</strong> Deliberation 171 also sets separate graded levels for individual care streams. Emergency care has four levels, from Niveau 1 (on-call doctors) to Niveau 4 (the CHT reference emergency service). Perinatal care has four levels, starting with the Centre de périnatalité de proximité (local perinatal center) and moving up through obstetric and neonatal units. Under Article 17, the government must fix by arrêté (decree) the niveau de soins (level of care) at which each authorized facility may operate within each scheme. These level decrees were not reviewed for this document. The Chambre territoriale des comptes reported in 2023 that the carte sanitaire and schemes had not been updated since 2016. Classify the CHT as “Territorial level — public reference hospital” and the CHS as “Territorial level — public specialized hospital (psychiatry/geriatrics).”
        </p>
        <ul data-info-section="role">
            <li>Serve as the reference hospital for the whole territory, receiving cases from the Centre hospitalier du Nord, the provincial medico-social centers and private practitioners</li>
            <li>Provide the only reference-level (Niveau 4) emergency service, the Service d’accueil et de traitement des urgences (SAU), together with emergency call regulation (centre 15)</li>
            <li>Organize medical evacuations (évacuations sanitaires) outside the territory, mainly to Australia and metropolitan France, for care not available locally</li>
            <li>The CHS Albert-Bousquet is the territorial reference hospital for psychiatry and also provides geriatric care.</li>
        </ul>
        <p data-info-section="services"><strong>Core Capability Areas</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Full range of medicine, surgery and obstetrics, intensive care, neonatal care, and the heavy equipment and high-cost activities subject to authorization under Deliberation 429, Article 2 (for example, cancer radiotherapy, hemodialysis and interventional procedures)</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Typical Bed Capacity</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>CHT (Médipôle): about 645 beds and places, including about 537 short-stay beds. CHS Albert-Bousquet: about 263 beds (psychiatry and geriatrics).</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="p-modal text-justify">
            <em><strong>Note:</strong> Legal basis: Délibération n° 429 of 3 November 1993, Article 2; Délibération n° 171 of 25 January 2001, Articles 11, 13 and 17 and Annex 2 (emergency scheme). Bed figures come from secondary sources (French Wikipedia, citing ISEE/INSEE) and should be checked against current DASS data.</em>
        </p>
